                        "glosses": [
                            "Sparkling."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "not-comparable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "13a18c59-5986-4150-8d4e-781eaa890d3f",
                                "sense_id": "00522b19-f307-3c0d-b650-f18c52d61a3c",
                                "text": "1909, Ralph Henry Barbour, The Lilac Girl, Fairfield, IA: 1st World Library, 2004, Chapter 10, p. 86,\nAnd when the sun shone against the walls of her palace it was filled with a lovely lavender light, and when the moon shone it was all asparkle with silver."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "76120215-7d65-4112-a83c-ef195f34acd4",
                                "sense_id": "00522b19-f307-3c0d-b650-f18c52d61a3c",
                                "text": "[…] the body of each high seat was a single jewel of monstrous size: the left-hand seat a black opal, asparkle with steel-blue fire, the next a fire-opal, as it were a burning coal, the third seat an alexandrite, purple like wine by night but deep sea-green by day."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5bc3ebf7-4eff-4a31-96b3-c1d83e915c1c",
                                "sense_id": "00522b19-f307-3c0d-b650-f18c52d61a3c",
                                "text": "Out on the deck, with the breeze blowing and the whole sea a-sparkle with light and movement, she felt little sickness at all;"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "30022005-d145-46bb-9bb1-35eab703695f",
                                "sense_id": "00522b19-f307-3c0d-b650-f18c52d61a3c",
                                "text": "[…] I kept hoping for the film to spin back to the moment where my aunt materialized asparkle with the gems previously the property of the rabbi's late wife."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
